<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vasily Dolgorukov-Krymsky</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1722">1722</a>–<a href="/years/1782">1782</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Vasily Mikhailovich Dolgorukov</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vasily Mikhailovich Dolgorukov was a Russian general who commanded the Second Army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1768-1774">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1768–1774. In 1771 he stormed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perekop">Perekop</a> lines and occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimean peninsula</a>, taking Kefe and other Ottoman fortresses, for which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a> gave him the honorific surname Krymsky ('of Crimea'). He later served as governor-general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vasily-dolgorukov-krymsky.jpg" alt="Vasily Dolgorukov-Krymsky" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Roslin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dolgorukov-Krymsky_Vasily_Michailovich.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born on 12 July 1722, the son of the senator Prince Mikhail Vladimirovich Dolgorukov and Princess Yevdokiya Yurievna Odoyevskaya. His childhood was overshadowed by the disgrace and imprisonment of his uncle Vasily Vladimirovich Dolgorukov under Empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-of-russia">Anna Ioannovna</a>, who barred the family from officer rank. He joined the army as a private in 1735, at 13, and fought under Field Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkhard-christoph-von-munnich">Burkhard Christoph von Münnich</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>. At the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perekop">Perekop</a> he was the first soldier to climb the walls alive and was made a poruchik, the only member of his clan exempted from the ban during Anna's reign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Under Empress Elizabeth Petrovna he rose quickly: captain in 1741, lieutenant-colonel and adjutant to his rehabilitated uncle, now president of the Collegium of War, by 1745, and colonel of the Tobolsk Infantry Regiment in 1747. As a major-general in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> he distinguished himself at the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kostrzyn-nad-odra">Küstrin</a> and the Battle of Zorndorf in August 1758, was wounded in the leg, and led an infantry brigade at Kunersdorf. Catherine the Great made him general-in-chief on the day of her coronation and gave him the Order of St. Andrew in 1767.</p><p>In 1769 he was sent to guard the border with the Crimean Khanate, and the next year he succeeded Petr Ivanovich Panin in command of the 2nd Field Army. In 1771 he took the fortifications of the Isthmus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perekop">Perekop</a> on 25 June and defeated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> and Turkish army at Kefe on 10 July, after which the Turks left Arabat, Yeni-Kale, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerch">Kerch</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaklava">Balaklava</a> and other towns. The Russian protégé <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahin-giray">Şahin Giray</a> became khan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>At the celebration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kucuk-kaynarca">Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca</a> in July 1775 he received a sword set with diamonds and the addition of Krymsky to his name. Resentful at not being made a field marshal, he retired to improve his estates near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, Znamenskoye-Gubailovo and Volynshchina. In charge of Moscow from 1780 until his death in 1782, he helped prepare the opening of the Moscow Governorate under Catherine's provincial reform and was respected for his honesty. He built the House of the Unions and other mansions of the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vasily Dolgorukov-Krymsky” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1761" aria-label="Moscow on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5370 263 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Vasily Dolgorukov-Krymsky"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5820 533h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1761">What was here in 1761? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
